<?xml version='1.0' encoding='UTF-8'?>
<worksheet xml:id="quiz-euler-hamiltonian-att-2">
  <title>Quiz: Euler and Hamiltonian Circuits Quiz Attempt 2</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Name: <fillin characters="45"/>
    </p>
    <p>Answer all four questions. Show your work and explain your reasoning. Crossings without a dot are not vertices.</p>
  </introduction>
  
  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>Determine whether the graph has a Hamiltonian circuit. Explain your reasoning. You may mark a circuit on the graph to support your answer.</p>
      <image source="images/form-2-bank-question-1" width="46%">
        <description>
          <p>An outer square and an inner square, with one vertex at each corner. Each square has its four boundary edges. The top-left, top-right, bottom-left, and bottom-right corners of the outer square are joined to the corresponding corners of the inner square. There are no other edges.</p>
        </description>
      </image>
    </statement>
  </exercise>
  
  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>Determine whether the graph has an Euler circuit. Justify your answer using the vertex degrees.</p>
      <image source="images/form-2-bank-question-6" width="46%">
        <description>
          <p>A rectangular grid with three columns and four rows of vertices. Edges join horizontally or vertically adjacent vertices. There are no diagonal edges.</p>
        </description>
      </image>
    </statement>
  </exercise>
  
  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>Find any Euler circuit in the graph. Give your answer as a sequence of vertices, starting and ending at the same vertex.</p>
      <image source="images/form-2-bank-question-7" width="52%">
        <description>
          <p>Vertices A, B, C, D, E, and F. The edges are AB, AC, AD, AF, BC, BD, BE, CE, and CF.</p>
        </description>
      </image>
    </statement>
  </exercise>
  
  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>Apply the nearest neighbor algorithm to the graph whose edge weights are given in the table, starting at vertex <m>A</m>. Give your answer as a sequence of vertices, starting and ending at vertex A.</p>
      <tabular halign="center" left="minor" right="minor" top="minor" bottom="minor">
        <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
          <cell/>
          <cell>
            <m>A</m>
          </cell>
          <cell>
            <m>B</m>
          </cell>
          <cell>
            <m>C</m>
          </cell>
          <cell>
            <m>D</m>
          </cell>
          <cell>
